    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in the high-acid stages. can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface when the pH is low?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin acts as an efficient emulsifier at low pH because its headgroup remains negatively charged, providing electrostatic repulsion between oil droplets even in acidic conditions. However, the increased ionic strength of the high-acid environment can sometimes shield these charges, requiring a formulation with sufficient lecithin to maintain steric stabilization and prevent coalescence. Its amphiphilic structure allows it to disrupt air bubbles lost during cooking while integrating into the fat matrix, though its effectiveness may be reduced compared to neutral pH systems without additional stabilizers like gums.",
